Preserve operation identity across crashes and old message lifetimes (@computing-message-lifetimes: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "Separate-eventual-confirmation-from-delay-and-throughput-guarantees.lingua#r_00DA585198FFD77571D6EDD999") { r_00F9BB17D72DBC9B162FFDFC4E
The server's atomic durable action is a contract, not an instruction supplied by the network. Reuse [Prove a copy and publish protocol under explicit persistence premises](Copy-persist-publish-and-acknowledge.lingua#r_00122E30DCA27BFCE26486692A) and [Recover a committed identity before deciding whether to retry](Recovery-request-identity-and-retry.lingua#r_00808A26E92166D742D797AA5D) to explain why the effect, identity and retained result must recover together. Those earlier lessons verify their own local representation. Our new record and client result journal require an appropriate persistence implementation before the abstract crash model becomes a claim about a real service. The present explorer has no torn writes: a crash event is a self-loop on already durable state.

Four original failures show why the contract matters. First, a receiver that adds the amount on every delivery applies request (1,2) twice after a lost reply, producing four instead of two. Second, a server that preserves total two but forgets identity one after a crash can accept the retry as new and also produce four. Third, a client that accepts any reply can treat an old result two as confirmation of a second job of five, producing reported results (2,2) although only the first job committed. Fourth, issuing a success reply before durable commit permits the client to confirm two while a crash leaves the server at zero. Each failure breaks a different clause of the invariant.

Now change the identity scheme to one alternating bit. Suppose the receiver expects bit zero. It accepts (0,2), then (1,5), and expects zero again. An indefinitely delayed duplicate of the first (0,2) can now masquerade as the next operation, even if the intended next amount is three. The accepted amounts become (2,5,2). Retaining just the last bit cannot distinguish this old packet from the new bit-zero position. This counterexample uses authentic unchanged messages and the already declared unbounded duplicate lifetime; no attacker or corruption is required.

A bounded sequence number does not by itself solve reuse. Our construction stops after identity 255. It can safely reject old values because it never returns to them. Extending service requires a specified new namespace or lifetime discipline, with a proof that old messages cannot be accepted as new operations. An epoch stored durably and carried in every request/reply is one possible design direction; it still needs uniqueness, exhaustion, retention and recovery rules. Choosing a random value supplies a probabilistic claim only after specifying its distribution and collision model.

Retaining only the most recent result is justified by the client rule: it never issues i+1 until it has durably accepted i. After moving forward, it cannot require another confirmation of i in this model. If recovery forgets that confirmation, the premise fails. If a different client can ask for old results, or the application wants an audit log, the service needs a different retention contract. Storage reclamation is therefore connected to what old participants and messages may still request, just as earlier slot reuse depended on the absence of surviving old writers.

Finally, a logical account is not the physical world. Publishing a local result cannot atomically guarantee that a separate actuator moved a piece. If movement occurs before publication, a crash can cause a repeated movement on retry; if publication occurs first, a crash can leave a recorded movement that never happened. A complete design needs a specified observable/idempotent operation or a coordination and recovery protocol for that external participant. The word exactly-once must name the effect and failure boundary it covers.

**Work:** replay the five failures, naming the first invalid state in each. Propose an epoch extension and identify its new durable fields before writing code. Explain what becomes unprovable if durable storage can roll back, replies can be forged, or old endpoints continue to send under the same namespace after replacement. These are concrete changes of model, not reasons to abandon the conditional proof.

**Continue:** [Separate eventual confirmation from delay and throughput guarantees](Separate-eventual-confirmation-from-delay-and-throughput-guarantees.lingua#r_00DA585198FFD77571D6EDD999).

**Return:** [Computing](../Computing.lingua#r_02FE84C805C79A9D396F478641).
